{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"personalizationThresholdSeconds","context":"# personalizationThresholdSeconds\n\nvar personalizationThresholdSeconds: Int?\n\nDefines the maximum duration of underfilled ad time (in seconds) allowed in an ad break. If the duration of underfilled ad time exceeds the personalization threshold, then the personalization of the ad break is abandoned and the underlying content is shown. This feature applies to *ad replacement* in live and VOD streams, rather than ad insertion, because it relies on an underlying content stream. For more information about ad break behavior, including ad replacement and insertion, see Ad Behavior in AWS Elemental MediaTailor.\n\nGenerated by\nDokka\n\n© 2026, Amazon Web Services, Inc. or its affiliates. All rights reserved","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdk-for-kotlin/api/latest/mediatailor/aws.sdk.kotlin.services.mediatailor.model/-playback-configuration/-builder/personalization-threshold-seconds.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"personalizationThresholdSeconds","context":"# personalizationThresholdSeconds\n\nvar personalizationThresholdSeconds: Int?\n\nDefines the maximum duration of underfilled ad time (in seconds) allowed in an ad break. If the duration of underfilled ad time exceeds the personalization threshold, then the personalization of the ad break is abandoned and the underlying content is shown. This feature applies to *ad replacement* in live and VOD streams, rather than ad insertion, because it relies on an underlying content stream. For more information about ad break behavior, including ad replacement and insertion, see Ad Behavior in AWS Elemental MediaTailor.\n\nGenerated by\nDokka\n\n© 2026, Amazon Web Services, Inc. or its affiliates. All rights reserved","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdk-for-kotlin/api/latest/mediatailor/aws.sdk.kotlin.services.mediatailor.model/-put-playback-configuration-response/-builder/personalization-threshold-seconds.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"personalizationThresholdSeconds","context":"# personalizationThresholdSeconds\n\nvar personalizationThresholdSeconds: Int?\n\nDefines the maximum duration of underfilled ad time (in seconds) allowed in an ad break. If the duration of underfilled ad time exceeds the personalization threshold, then the personalization of the ad break is abandoned and the underlying content is shown. This feature applies to *ad replacement* in live and VOD streams, rather than ad insertion, because it relies on an underlying content stream. For more information about ad break behavior, including ad replacement and insertion, see Ad Behavior in AWS Elemental MediaTailor.\n\nGenerated by\nDokka\n\n© 2026, Amazon Web Services, Inc. or its affiliates. All rights reserved","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdk-for-kotlin/api/latest/mediatailor/aws.sdk.kotlin.services.mediatailor.model/-get-playback-configuration-response/-builder/personalization-threshold-seconds.html"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS Elemental MediaTailor now offers Yield Optimization to automatically fill ad breaks with Amazon Ads demand - AWS","context":"AWS Elemental MediaTailor has introduced a new feature called Yield Optimization, which automatically monetizes unused ad inventory during server-side ad insertion (SSAI) for livestreams. This capability is exclusive to publishers in the Amazon Publisher Services (APS) Streaming TV program and comes at no additional cost. Yield optimization turns unfilled ad breaks into monetized impressions, enabling publishers to maximize their revenue without requiring any extra infrastructure or client-side changes.\n\nThe feature is designed for large-scale live events, ensuring low latency and fast response times during peak viewership. Ads are stitched directly into the stream, ensuring seamless playback on every device. Publishers have control over which ads run in their streams by setting their own price floors and category rules.\n\nYield optimization is available in all AWS Regions where MediaTailor is available, including US East (Ohio), US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), Africa (Cape Town), Asia Pacific (Hyderabad, Malaysia, Melbourne, Mumbai, Osaka, Seoul, Singapore, Sydney, Tokyo), Canada (Central), Europe (Frankfurt, Ireland, London, Paris, Stockholm), Middle East (UAE), and South America (São Paulo). Publishers can monitor ad fill rate improvements using MediaTailor metrics in Amazon CloudWatch and track revenue and earnings through the APS Publisher portal","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/mediatailor-yield-optimization/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS Elemental MediaTailor adds configurable ad timeout and concurrency controls for improved ad fill and faster startup - AWS","context":"AWS Elemental MediaTailor, a service offered by Amazon Web Services (AWS), has introduced new features that allow users to configure ad decision server (ADS) timeouts and concurrency controls. Previously, these settings required contacting AWS Support for modifications. The new settings include individual HTTP ad request timeouts, total ad personalization time budgets for live, VOD, and live ad prefetch, and the ability to enable parallel ADS requests. These adjustments enable users to optimize ad delivery performance for their specific workflows. For instance, increasing the personalization time budget for live events can enhance ad fill rates, while enabling parallel ADS requests in VOD workflows can decrease overall response time for faster video startup. Additionally, new prefetch-specific timeout settings offer more granularity for livestream ad retrieval. Users can configure these settings through the AWS Elemental MediaTailor console, AWS CLI, or AWS SDKs using the new AdsPersonalizationTimeouts and AdsPersonalizationConcurrency parameters on playback configurations. This feature is accessible in all AWS Regions where AWS Elemental MediaTailor is available. For more information on configuring ADS request timeouts, personalization time budgets, and concurrency, refer to the Advanced settings section in the AWS Elemental MediaTailor User Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/mediatail-configurable-ad-timeout-and-concurrency/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS Elemental MediaTailor Monetization Functions adds ad response hooks - AWS","context":"AWS Elemental MediaTailor, a video service offered by Amazon Web Services (AWS), has introduced two new monetization functions: post ad decision server (ADS) response and pre manifest insertion. MediaTailor personalizes and inserts ads into live and on-demand streams, and these functions allow customers to run custom logic at defined points in an ad-personalized playback session. The post ADS response hook runs after MediaTailor parses an ADS response and resolves all VAST wrappers, enabling customers to call a secondary ad source when the primary one returns insufficient demand, remove disallowed ads based on content or brand policies, and add house ads or promotions from their marketing systems. The pre manifest insertion hook runs at the last point before MediaTailor returns the ad pod to the viewer, allowing customers to make a final check on what is about to play and insert personalized slates when necessary. Both hooks are fail-open, meaning they discard the function output and continue with default ad insertion on a timeout, expression error, or resource limit. These hooks are available in all AWS Regions where MediaTailor is available. To learn more, customers can refer to the Monetization Functions section in the AWS Elemental MediaTailor User Guide and the MediaTailor pricing page. To get started, they should sign in to the AWS Elemental MediaTailor console. MediaTailor is available in all AWS Regions where it is offered. For a complete list, customers can check the AWS Regional Services List","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/aws-elemental-mediatailor-functions-ad-response-hooks/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-ai-ml","skill_description":"Selects, deploys, and customizes AI models on Amazon SageMaker. Training or Processing jobs, fine-tuning (SFT/DPO/RLVR/RLAIF), model selection, dataset preparation, evaluation, SageMaker or Bedrock deployment, inference optimization and endpoint diagnostics. Covers the full lifecycle from planning through production. Use when fine-tuning models on SageMaker, choosing which base model to customize, fine-tune, or deploy from SageMaker JumpStart or Hub, SageMakerPublicHub, or the SageMaker public model catalog, transforming or validating training data, evaluating model quality, deploying or optimizing endpoints, configuring IAM/S3 for training, or managing SageMaker Managed MLflow. Use for endpoint health, failures, latency, logs, metrics, errors. Covers Serverless Model Customization, Nova and OSS deployment paths, and PySDK v3. NOT for Ground Truth labeling, Feature Store, or general-purpose AWS infrastructure.\n\nServices: sagemaker, s3, iam, bedrock\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, data-scientist\nWorkload: ai-ml\n*","skill_name":"aws-ai-ml"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"cloudfront","skill_description":"Configures Amazon CloudFront content delivery across six workflows: when to use CloudFront and how it fits with AWS WAF, Shield, CloudFront Functions, Lambda@Edge, Route 53, and origins (creating a distribution, caching, and Flat Rate Pricing (FRP) versus pay-as-you-go pricing); managing custom-domain TLS certificates (ACM in us-east-1); configuring multi-tenant distributions; protecting origins with origin access control (OAC), VPC origins, and origin mutual TLS (mTLS); securing content with signed URLs and cookies, geographic restrictions, viewer mutual TLS, and edge token validation; and observing traffic with standard and real-time logs. Applicable when the customer wants to put CloudFront in front of content, choose pricing, lock an origin, restrict who can view content, or analyze logs. Not applicable for the Route 53 DNS side of a CloudFront custom domain or failover between distributions, or for pure-Route 53 DNS work (see the route53 skill).\n\n\nServices: cloudfront, s3, acm, vpc\nTasks: configure, secure, deploy\nPersona: developer, devops, sre\nWorkload: networking, content-delivery, security\n*","skill_name":"cloudfront"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Look up AWS MediaTailor docs on PersonalizationThresholdSeconds ad break behavior"}
